  "query_preview": "..\n(This evening through next Monday)\nIssued at 1206 PM CDT Tue Sep 15 2026\n\nOur prolonged, unseasonably warm weather pattern continues once\nagain today with sunny skies and temperatures currently spanning\nthe 90s across the Mid-South. Unfortunately, little to no relief\nis in sight for the foreseeable future, with a dry forecast\npersisting until at least the upcoming weekend. The high pressure\nsystem driving this pattern will continue to strengthen over the\nnext few days, with high temperatures remaining in the upper 90s\nto near 100 degrees through the rest of the week. Dew point\ntemperatures will generally remain in the upper 60s to low 70s in\nthe afternoons, leading to heat index values hovering right\naround to above Heat Advisory criteria (105+). With increased\nconfidence in our heat index values for tomorrow, the current\nHeat Advisory was extended through Wednesday evening. An\nadditional extension into Thursday and / or Friday will likely be\nwarranted for some of the Mid-South, but confidence is low in the\nexact location. Thus, will handle further extensions with later\nforecast packages. The compilation of upper 90 degree high \ntemperatures, heat indices at or above 105 degrees, and long- \nduration of these temperatures has led to much of the area being \nincluded in a major to extreme heat risk. While overnight \ntemperatures have been dipping back to the low to mid 70s, extra \nprecautions should be taken through the daytime hours and \nespecially during peak heating hours. Be sure to continue to drink\nplenty of fluids and take frequent breaks if you must be outside \nin the afternoon hours.\n\nBy the beginning of next week, a weak upper-level trough will\nmove across the Plains with another weak low meandering through\nthe Gulf. Both of these systems will likely aid in breaking us\nout of the high pressure stronghold we have been in, although the\ntiming is still questionable. The majority of ensemble guidance\nat this point has the temperature spread growing greatly by next\nMonday onward (around a 10 degree spread), thus suggesting\noverall low confidence in the forecast into next week. While a\nforecast change would certainly be welcome, we are certainly not\nout of the woods yet with the continued, oppressive heat.",
